 "literal": "Forest elephant bark desiring ate / scarred-trunked ōmai tree wind-beaten long branch / waving dry branch having climbed quickly / loneliness-giving voice dove female calling / desert path reached beautiful-habitation small village / stayed they whether he-himself? We to him / 'we agree not' said fault-for / without saying departing capable-he",
 "translation": "Did he stay in that small, beautiful village near the desert path, where a male dove climbs upon the waving, dry, wind-beaten long branch of the scarred-trunked ōmai tree—whose bark was stripped and eaten by a forest elephant with desire—and calls out to its female mate with a lonely voice? He, who was capable of departing without a word, to punish me for the fault of saying 'I do not consent'?",
 "modern": "காட்டு யானை தன் விருப்பத்தோடு பட்டையை உரித்துத் தின்ற, பொரிந்த அடிமரத்தைக் கொண்ட ஓமை மரத்தின், காற்று மோதும் நீண்ட அசையும் உலர்ந்த கிளையின் மேல் ஏறி, ஆண் புறா தன் துணையைத் தனிமை தரும் குரலில் அழைக்கும் பாலைநில வழியில் உள்ள அழகிய குடில்களைக் கொண்ட சிறு ஊரில் அவர் தங்கிவிட்டாரோ? 'பிரிவுக்கு உடன்பட மாட்டேன்' என்று நாம் அவரிடம் கூறிய குற்றத்திற்காக, நம்மிடம் சொல்லாமலேயே பிரிந்து செல்ல வல்லமை படைத்தவர் அவர்.",
 "explanation": "The heroine speaks to herself or her friend in deep grief after her lover has departed for the desert path to earn wealth. She wonders if he has halted at a small village along the harsh wasteland. She describes the landscape where a forest elephant has stripped the bark of an ōmai tree, and on its wind-beaten, dry branch, a male dove calls out to its mate with a lonely, sorrowful voice. This image of the dove calling its mate mirrors her own loneliness and longing for her partner. She bitterly notes that he was capable of leaving her without even saying goodbye, as if to punish her for previously refusing to consent to his departure.",
